Q: WHAT IS A REAL ESTATE INVESTMENT TRUST, OR REIT?

A: In general, a REIT is an entity that:

   - combines the capital of many investors to acquire or provide financing for
     real estate;

   - offers the benefits of a diversified portfolio of real estate assets under
     professional management;

   - is able to qualify as a "real estate investment trust" for U.S. federal
     income tax purposes and is therefore not subject to federal corporate
     income taxes on its net income, which substantially eliminates the "double
     taxation" (i.e., taxation at both the corporate and shareholder levels)
     that generally results from investments in a corporation; and

   - must pay dividends to investors of at least 90% of its taxable income.

   In this prospectus, we refer to an entity that qualifies as a real estate
   investment trust for U.S. federal income tax purposes as a "REIT."

