  IN CERTAIN CIRCUMSTANCES, WE MAY BE SUBJECT TO FEDERAL AND STATE INCOME TAXES
  AS A REIT OR OTHER STATE OR LOCAL INCOME TAXES, WHICH WOULD REDUCE OUR CASH
  AVAILABLE TO PAY DIVIDENDS TO OUR SHAREHOLDERS.

     Even if we qualify and maintain our status as a REIT, we may be subject to
federal income taxes or state taxes. For example, if we have net income from a
"prohibited transaction," such income will be subject to a 100% tax. We may not
be able to make sufficient distributions to avoid the 4% excise tax that
generally applies to income retained by a REIT. We may also decide to retain
income we earn from the sale or other disposition of our property and pay income
tax directly on such income. In that event, our shareholders would be treated as
if they earned that income and paid the tax on it directly. However,

